From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:04:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: pad the decoded stride to 64 bytes

The packed 10-bit capture formats carry five bytes per four samples, so
their stride is width * 5 / 4 and is not a multiple of 64 even though
every coded format aligns the width to 64: a 1920-wide NV15 frame gets a
2400-byte stride, and 2400 % 64 is 32.

The decoder does not care. It programs every stride register as
bytesperline / 16 -- y_hor_virstride and uv_hor_virstride for all three
VDPU381 backends, and the VP9 reference strides -- and width * 5 / 4 on
a 64-aligned width is always a multiple of 16.

The RGA 2D engine does care, and it is the consumer that matters: it
rejects a blit whose source stride is not 64-byte aligned, and it is the
only path from a packed 10-bit decoded frame to any other pixel format.
Without this, hardware scaling, colour conversion and blending are
unavailable for exactly the widths that are not multiples of 256 --
1920 among them, so 1080p 10-bit HEVC has no hardware filter path at
all, while 4K works because 3840 * 5 / 4 happens to land on 4800.

Pad the stride and scale sizeimage with it, before colmv_offset is taken
so the motion-vector area still follows the frame.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c   | 23 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
index 358502319..00c5ae308 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
@@ -104,12 +104,31 @@ static void rkvdec_fill_decoded_pixfmt(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx,
 				       struct v4l2_pix_format_mplane *pix_mp)
 {
 	const struct rkvdec_variant *variant = ctx->dev->variant;
+	struct v4l2_plane_pix_format *plane = &pix_mp->plane_fmt[0];
+	u32 aligned_bpl;
 
 	v4l2_fill_pixfmt_mp(pix_mp, pix_mp->pixelformat, pix_mp->width, pix_mp->height);
 
-	ctx->colmv_offset = pix_mp->plane_fmt[0].sizeimage;
+	/*
+	 * Pad the stride to 64 bytes. Every coded format sets step_width = 64,
+	 * so an 8-bit stride is already a multiple of 64 and this is a no-op;
+	 * the packed 10-bit formats are what it reaches, because NV15 and NV20
+	 * carry five bytes per four samples and 1920 * 5 / 4 is 2400, not a
+	 * multiple of 64. The decoder itself is indifferent -- it takes every
+	 * stride register as bytesperline / 16 -- but the RGA 2D engine
+	 * requires a 64-byte-aligned stride to blit these buffers, and it is
+	 * the only route from a packed 10-bit decoded frame to any other
+	 * format.
+	 */
+	aligned_bpl = ALIGN(plane->bytesperline, 64);
+	if (aligned_bpl != plane->bytesperline) {
+		plane->sizeimage = plane->sizeimage / plane->bytesperline * aligned_bpl;
+		plane->bytesperline = aligned_bpl;
+	}
+
+	ctx->colmv_offset = plane->sizeimage;
 
-	pix_mp->plane_fmt[0].sizeimage += variant->ops->colmv_size(pix_mp->width, pix_mp->height);
+	plane->sizeimage += variant->ops->colmv_size(pix_mp->width, pix_mp->height);
 }
 
 static void rkvdec_reset_fmt(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx, struct v4l2_format *f,
